# kmod -- insmod, rmmod, lsmod, modinfo, modprobe and depmod, all one binary.
#
# The live system needs it because the kernel ships most of its drivers as
# modules: without depmod there is no modules.dep, and without modules.dep
# modprobe cannot resolve a driver's dependencies, so a machine whose disk
# controller is a module never finds its own root filesystem.
#
# kmod 33 is the version pinned here rather than something newer because it is
# the last release that still ships an autotools configure. Later ones are
# meson-only, and meson is not packaged -- ninja is, but meson itself is a
# Python program with its own dependency set, and importing it to build one
# 200 KB tool would be a poor trade.
#
# Not an LFS package, so it is pinned here rather than in versions.env.
SRC_URL=https://www.kernel.org/pub/linux/utils/kernel/kmod/kmod-33.tar.xz
SRC_SHA256=dc768b3155172091f56dc69430b5481f2d76ecd9ccb54ead8c2540dbcf5ea9bc
SRC_DIR=kmod-33

# --with-module-directory is load-bearing and easy to get wrong. Duct is
# merged-/usr, so modules live at /usr/lib/modules; the kernel's own
# modules_install is told to match. Leaving kmod on its /lib/modules default
# works by accident through the /lib -> usr/lib symlink, right up until depmod
# is invoked with -b against a staging root where that symlink does not exist
# yet -- and then it silently finds no modules and writes an empty
# modules.dep.
#
# Manpages need xsltproc and the DocBook stylesheets, neither of which is
# packaged; the tools are self-describing enough to live without them for now.
CONFIGURE_ARGS="--disable-nls --disable-static --disable-manpages \
	--with-module-directory=/usr/lib/modules \
	--with-zlib --with-xz --without-zstd --without-openssl"
